From 463f81075ff0e78687a9bc2005a20cde8b56dc77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 07:30:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08043/11362] repeat_penalty triples the rate of valid engines
 and halves the cost -- and does not make them better

CONFIRMED for engine VALIDITY and COST; NOT confirmed for engine QUALITY. 36 attempts matched by
game, seed and temperature, six games, both arms through ONE server process per GPU, scored
OUT-OF-SAMPLE for the first time on this path.

What is real and significant. The shipped path accepted a mechanically defective candidate in
22 of 36 attempts -- Phase 2's 13-of-15, reproduced at larger n on independent samples. Adding
`repeat_penalty 1.1` + one plain re-ask takes usable engines from 13/36 to 22/36, 13 paired wins
against 4 losses, exact two-sided sign test p = 0.049. It is also CHEAPER: 47.2 s per attempt for
round 0 against the control's 100.3 s, 65.2 s even charging the re-ask, and the 4096-token cap is
hit 2 times in 36 instead of 20. The mechanism is visible in the defect census -- `missing_return`
falls 13 -> 2 -- so the model is finishing the function instead of rambling into a comment wall,
which is exactly what a repetition penalty should do. `repeat_penalty` carries 11 of the 13 wins;
the re-ask fired 9 times, rescued 2, and adds 2 wins against 0 losses (p = 0.5).

What is NOT real. The strict out-of-sample quality funnel moves 2 of 6 games to 3 of 6 -- one
game, ft09, one attempt -- and the paired quality test is 3 against 2, p = 1.000. With only 5
discordant pairs the best p that comparison could ever have reached is 0.0625, so it could not
have shown an effect at this n; that is said here and not offered later as an excuse. On tu93 and
sc25 the best over EVERY scored completion in every arm is 0 of 19 and 0 of 1 held-out changing
transitions correct. Nothing produced a good engine on those games, at any budget, ever.

Usable is not good, and out-of-sample says so far more sharply than in-sample did. On ft09 the
treatment is usable in 6 of 6 attempts, and in 5 of those 6 the engine hallucinates a change on 12
to 16 of the 16 unseen INERT clicks. It learned that clicks do something; it did not learn where
they have to land. A funnel counted at `usable` scores ft09 6/6; the honest count is 1/6.

My own pre-registered quality bar admitted junk, which is the most useful thing here. At
`heldout_cell_recall > 0.5`, tu93's best CONTROL engine passes with 0.537 while getting 0 of 19
held-out transitions correct and inventing 82 cells -- per-cell overlap rewards smearing a
roughly-right change over roughly-right places. The strict bar reported throughout (at least one
held-out changing transition predicted EXACTLY, falling back to zero hallucinated no-ops where
nothing changing is held out) is not gameable that way and excludes it. Both bars are in the
payload; the recall bar is kept because its failure is a result.

Held narrow on purpose: this run's control is much better than Phase 2's arm A (13/36 vs 1/15),
partly because its dry run sees only the 8 SHOWN rows to keep the held-out set clean while Phase
2's saw all 25. The two are NOT comparable and the valid comparison is the internal one.

Nothing is wired in. arc_executable_world_model.py is untouched and no threshold was read, changed
or written. Generator proven not assumed: CUDA build from /proc/<pid>/exe, 21416 MiB resident per
card on distinct bus IDs, n_ctx 32768 read back from /props, non-default ports.
